{"sample_id": 40, "prompt": "After we left Zihuatenajo, needed fuel and water so we took a left turn in", "reference": " to Acapulco Bay. The Yacht Club is on the wind protected left side of the big bay, the rest of the shoreline looks like Miami, or Ft. Lauderdale, or Puerto Vallarta. Our first impression was to get what we needed and get out. It was crowded boats on the hook, mooring balls, and a lot of crap tied around the area. We found the marina and fuel docks, met with the management of the thing to get permission to buy some fuel and did just that. They didn\u2019t even have any potable (drinking) water at the dock. (so they said). Turns out it is harder than you would think to find potable water from a hose. Normally, there is drinking water accessible via a water hose just like you have attached to your house. There isn\u2019t a fee for the water since you are purchasing fuel. But we found that once we left Puerto Vallarta, we couldn\u2019t find water until we were so far south that we were almost to Guatamala. The snooty factor in Acapulco is about a 9 on the 10 point snooty scale.\nDidn\u2019t even drag a camera out.\nAcapulco helds no interest for us\u2026so goodbye Acapulco.", "generated_dual_stream": "the road. The road was a gravel road in some cases with a lot of people who had no idea how to drive it. They were puzzled and run off ledges and would have been up to the mountains on the road and over what to do", "generated_original": "the main street. We had a couple of other options, but this seemed the most logical. At the next intersection, we turned right, and drove down the street. It took us about 10 minutes to find the turnoff to La Mina", "metrics_dual_stream": {"rouge": {"rouge1": 0.20634920634920634, "rouge2": 0.023999999999999997, "rougeL": 0.11111111111111113, "rougeLsum": 0.12698412698412698}, "bleu": {"bleu": 0.0, "precisions": [0.5714285714285714, 0.10416666666666667, 0.02127659574468085, 0.0], "brevity_penalty": 0.026445928585926707, 