{"sample_id": 470, "prompt": "Question: How to identify new species using molecular phylogenetics?\nHi! My current task is", "reference": " to identify fungal strain which genome has been sequenced. To do so I've built several phylogenetic trees (single-gene: 18s SSU, 28s LSU, ITS and multi-gene: ITS + 28s LSU + RPB2). Data set for such analysis contained sequences from two orders (BLAST search showed affiliation with this two orders). Topology of trees tells that target strain may be a new species or may be insertae sedis.\nWhat kind of analysis should be performed in order to proof that target strain is a new species or may be insertae sedis or none of that?\nOne of the multi-gene trees for one of the orders is shown on picture.", "generated_dual_stream": "to identify new species and we would like to introduce you to a new method of using molecular phylogenetics. Create your own image of the real-world situations involving new species. This will be used to determine which species are and are not related to the", "generated_original": "to identify new species using molecular phylogenetics. I'm pretty new to the field and I would like to know more about the steps and how to do this. For example, how do I select a reference genome sequence? How do I identify hom", "metrics_dual_stream": {"rouge": {"rouge1": 0.2193548387096774, "rouge2": 0.06535947712418301, "rougeL": 0.14193548387096774, "rougeLsum": 0.1806451612903226}, "bleu": {"bleu": 0.0, "precisions": [0.3617021276595745, 0.10869565217391304, 0.0, 0.0], "brevity_penalty": 0.20711769237138883, "length_ratio": 0.3884297520661157, "translation_length": 47, "reference_length": 121}, "bert_score_precision": 0.8504444360733032, "bert_score_recall": 0.8031742572784424, "bert_score_f1": 0.8261337280273438}, "metrics_original": {"rouge": {"rouge1": 0.20915032679738563, "rouge2": 0.039735099337748346, "rougeL": 0.09150326797385622, "rougeLsum": 0.14379084967320263}, "bleu": {"bleu": 0.0, "precisions": [0.3617021276595745,