{"sample_id": 995, "prompt": "Cardamom also known as elaichi, has a wonderful aroma and an enticing warm, spicy", "reference": "-sweet flavour. The whole pods can be added to dishes, or the seeds can be extracted and either added whole or ground. When using cardamom to flavour curries, lightly crush the whole pod and it to the dish, and remember to use the whole pods for garnishing to add that dash of green. But remember to use it sparingly, as a little cardamom goes a long way.\nCombating Cold and Flu: If you are suffering from a flu, black cardamoms form the perfect soother. Just steep some pods in water with honey and it will relieve you effectively.\nAids Digestion: Its strong aroma helps in activating our taste and sensory elements, thereby aiding digestion. So, after a heavy meal keep some cardamoms by your side.\nMouth freshener: The floral and sweet aroma of cardamoms make it a natural breath freshener and helps promote oral hygiene.\nLowers high blood pressure: According to Ayurveda a concoction of a teaspoon coriander and one pinch of cardamom mixed with 1 cup of freshly squeezed peach juice can help bring down high blood pressure. Therefore, it is known to be a heart-friendly spice.\nSecret to good health: The Chinese believe drinking cardamom tea helps flushing out the toxins and keeps you healthy longer.\nSo, add a cardamom to your daily cup of tea, coffee, milk or a glass of warm water and honey, along with adding it to your sweet dishes and savoury ones alike, to make the most of this wonderful spice.", "generated_dual_stream": "flavor. It is one of the oldest spices and is known to have been cultivated for over 3000 years. It can be traced back to the Greek ancient Egyptians and it is also cultivated in India, where people started to grow it in the seeds", "generated_original": "flavor. It\u2019s a key ingredient in Indian cuisine, as well as the Middle East, and is also used in many European recipes. It is also a great addition to your spice rack, and is wonderful in a variety of baked goods.", "metrics_dual_stream": {"rouge": {"rouge1": 0.1496598639455782, "rouge2": 0.04794520547945205, "rougeL": 0.09523809523809523, "rougeLsum": 0.12244897959183673}, "bleu": {"bleu": 0.0, "precisions": [0.4791666666666667, 0.14893617021276595, 0.021739130434782608, 0.0], "brevity_penalty": 0.008127515489292218, "length_ratio": 0.17204301075268819, "translation_length": 48, "reference_length": 279}, "bert_score_precision": 0.8336522579193115, "bert_score_recall": 0.7896987199783325, "bert_score_f1": 0.8110804557800293}, "metrics_original": {"rouge": {"rouge1": 0.11683848797250859, "rouge2": 0.01384083044982699, "rougeL": 0.08247422680412372, "rougeLsum": 0.09621993127147765}, "bleu": {"bleu": 0.0, "precisions": [0.44680851063829785, 0.06521739130434782, 0.0, 0.0], "brevity_penalty": 0.007182051498085687, "length_ratio": 0.16845878136200718, "translation_length": 47, "reference_length": 279}, "bert_score_precision": 0.8592398166656494, "bert_score_recall": 0.7994388937950134, "bert_score_f1": 0.8282613158226013}, "original_model_loss_on_full_text": 2.5143821239471436}
